Falsifies `paging::aarch64::the_two_halves_are_disjoint`.

The defect: the low half is written as a range ending at the kernel base, inclusive. Range form
is how most kernels spell this test ("user space is everything below the kernel"), and `<=`
against `>=` at a boundary is the oldest off-by-one there is. The single address
`0xffff_0000_0000_0000` is then in both halves at once, so a `Mapper` built for the low half
accepts the first page of the kernel's own address space.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!(Aarch64::is_in_half(Half::Low, va) && Aarch64::is_in_half(Half::High,
va)))`, at exactly `va == 0xffff_0000_0000_0000`. One witness, and the harness is over a fully
symbolic `va`, so the solver is free to find it.

**The half test is shared and the proof is not.** `PageFormat::is_in_half` is one default method in
`lib.rs`. The `sv39` record moves the sign bit and the `x86_64` one turns the equality into a
fits-in-the-mask test, so the three break it in three ways.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -487,7 +487,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
     fn is_in_half(half: Half, va: u64) -> bool {
         let top = va >> Self::SPLIT_SHIFT;
         match half {
-            Half::Low => top == 0,
+            Half::Low => va <= Self::half_base(Half::High),
             Half::High => top == (u64::MAX >> Self::SPLIT_SHIFT),
         }
     }
